 { The last topic we'll cover is the exchange sacrifice. This is perhaps the most common positional sacrifice, as there are many reasons and situations where it is justified. Let's begin with a typical example. } 1. { -KEY- } Rxe6 $1 { The best move. White gets three major advantages: damaging the structure, winning the bishop pair, and long-term dominance over the light squares. } fxe6 2. Qe2 $1 { The point of this move is not so much to win the pawn on e6 immediately, but to stop the ...e5 pawn break. } Kh8 3. Nf3 $1 { Another benefit of such a sacrifice is that the e5 square becomes an ideal outpost for a knight. } Nd5 4. Ng5 $18 ( 4. Qxe6 { was also strong, but the threat of capturing with the knight is even better as it will come with a fork. } ) 
Nc7 5. h4 $1 { Exposing the drawback of 1...fxe6. Black's kingside pawn chain is fragile. White is only a tempo or two away from a lethal attack. } ( 5. Nxe6 $2 { is again cashing in too early, when after } Nxe6 6. Qxe6 Qd6 $13 { the position is unclear and approximately equal. Black's extra rook offsets White's bishop pair and pawn. } ) 
Bf6 6. Nf3 $16 { There are many ways to continue, but a calm retreat of the knight works well. White can afford to be patient as Black does not have any active plan. They lack open files for their rooks, and there is also no attractive pawn break for them to aim for. White stands much better, and most human players here will panic as Black, leading to fresh opportunities. } 
