 ( { The possibility of an exchange sacrifice arose in the online game Carlsen, M - Hakobyan, A (2023). Do you think that playing } 1. Rxe6 { is best, or should you continue more slowly with } 1. Bd2 $2 ) 
1. { -KEY- } Rxe6 $1 { Correct! Magnus judges that the exchange sacrifice is worthwhile, with a concrete follow-up in mind. } ( 1. Bd2 $6 { leads to equality after } Nxc3 2. bxc3 Bxb3 3. axb3 Qd7 $11 { The reduced material and similar structure makes for a dry, equal middlegame. } ) 
fxe6 2. Qd3 $1 { The most precise follow-up. White's queen rushes to the light squares and takes aim along the weakened b1-h7 diagonal. Moves like 3.Qe4 or 3.Bxh6 are big threats. } Ncb4 { Targeting the queen and overprotecting d5. } 3. Qg6 $1 Bf6 4. Bxh6 { The pressure allows White to recover a pawn. Now the material advantage for Black is minimal. At the same time, Magnus has kept the usual positional pluses of bishop pair, better structure, and control over the light squares. } Qd7 5. Ne4 Qf7 6. Qg4 $1 { Keeping the queens on the board makes sense, since the Black king is weak. } Nc6 7. Be3 { Similar to the first example, White takes a patient approach. Black has little activity for his rooks and no pawn breaks to stir up counterplay. } Rad8 8. Rc1 Nce7 9. Nxf6+ $1 Qxf6 ( 9... Nxf6 { Trading queens doesn't help Black. } 10. Qxe6 Qxe6 11. Bxe6 Kh8 12. Ng5 $18 { Black gets overwhelmed by the threats. } ) 
10. Bg5 $1 Qf5 { Offering a queen trade to ease the pressure against the e6 pawn. } 11. Qxf5 exf5 12. Rxc7 $1 { Tactical vision! The knight on d5 is pinned. When the side that sacrifices an exchange is able to win back two pawns, it's usually game over. } Kh7 13. Rc5 ( 13. Bxd5 $1 { was easiest, since after } Nxd5 14. Bxd8 Nxc7 15. Bxc7 $18 { White has a huge material advantage. } ) 
Rd6 14. Bxe7 Nxe7 15. Ng5+ ( { Black resigned, since they are losing a rook. If } 15. Ng5+ Kh8 { or } Kh6 { there is } 16. Nf7 { . If } Kg6 { then } 16. Bf7 { decides the game. } ) 

