 { Another common situation where sacrificing the exchange can be necessary is in order to make a decisive breakthrough. } 2. { -KEY- } Rxd4 $1 exd4 3. Qxd4 { The rook on f6 is completely paralysed, making White's sacrifice mostly risk-free. } Qa4 { Seeking counterplay. } 4. Qd3 Qd7 ( { A forced retreat, to be able to meet } 4... Qd7 4. g5 { with } Rxf5 { . } ) 
 ( 4... Qxa5 $2 5. g5 $18 { wins the rook. } ) 
5. Qe4 Kg8 { Black has no useful moves, so they try to bring the king over to f8. } 6. Qe7 $1 { Breaking in on the 7th rank. } Qa4 ( 6... Qxe7 7. Rxe7 $18 { is hopeless. } ) 
7. g5 $1 { Other ways to win also work, but this is cleanest. } Rxf5 8. g6 Rf8 ( 8... Rxd5 { walks into } 9. gxf7 Kh7 10. Qe4 Kh8 { when the fastest win is } 11. Qe8 $1 Rxe8 12. fxe8=Q { White will emerge a rook up. } ) 
9. gxf7+ R8xf7 { Forced, since moving the king would have led to 10.Qxf8. } 10. Qxd6 $18 { Black's pieces are tied down, while White is in effect up material since they can recover the pinned rook on f7 whenever they want. } 
