 { A related concept to breaking through is to find a way to simplify the position (reduce material) by sacrificing the exchange. Let's take a look at an advanced example of this. } 1. { -KEY- } b3 $1 { A surprising move, giving up the exchange on purpose. } ( 1. Re1 $2 { The problem for White is that if they were to go chasing the e3 pawn, then Black would play } Rd7 $1 { when after } 2. Rxd7 Bxd7 3. Nxe3 Kg7 $11 { Black may be down a pawn but their piece activity has improved a lot, making the position around equal. } ) 
Bb2+ { Black has nothing better than to grab the exchange, but now White simplifies the position and at the same time will soon set up an ideal piece configuration, paralysing the opponent. This topic is discussed in more detail in the 'Endgame Play' chapter. } ( 1... Rd7 $2 { Now this move fails to } 2. Rxd7 Bxd7 3. Nxd4 exd4 4. Rxd4 $18 { White picks up both the d- and e-pawns. } ) 
2. Kxb2 Rxd1 3. Nxe3 { White is down an exchange, but has better pieces and a dangerous 3 vs 1 pawn majority on the queenside. } Rd7 { Exchanging is Black's best bet, since in general the side with an extra minor piece is benefited by having one rook on the board. } 4. Rxd7 Bxd7 5. h6 $1 { This is the paralysing configuration that White had in mind when playing the move 1.b3. Now, the black king is unable to get into the game. } Rg8 { Black has to escape the bind and chase after one of White's kingside pawns. } ( { The slower } 5... Be6 { fails to } 6. c3 $18 { when after } bxc3 7. Kxc3 $18 { White's connected passed pawns will decide the game. } ) 
6. Nd5 $1 { The f7 pawn is not important. What matters is eliminating the b4 pawn to start running the passed pawns. } Rg6 7. Nxb4 Rxh6 8. a4 Rg6 9. a5 h5 ( 9... Bc8 { Giving up a piece immediately will not save Black. } 10. a6 Bxa6 11. Bxa6 h5 12. Bc8 $18 { The bishop will sacrifice itself for the h-pawn at the right moment, while White is ready to start pushing the c-pawn. } ) 
10. Bxf7 Rh6 { Otherwise the h5 pawn falls. } 11. a6 Bc8 12. a7 Bb7 13. Bd5 $1 { The bishops are forced off, and the pawn will promote. This was a very advanced example. Besides the original idea, White also had to spot the theme of paralysis with 5.h6, as well as calculating the pawn race until the end. Still, I couldn't resist including it, as it contained an unusual mix of instructive themes. } 
