 { Another good reason to sacrifice an exchange is to improve your pawn structure. In the current position, can you find the best move for White? } 1. { -KEY- } Rxe5 $1 ( 1. fxe5 $2 { was Carlsen's choice in the online game Carlsen,M - Jobava,B (2023). However, it is much less precise since after } Nxe4 $1 2. Rxe4 Rc8 $1 { The problem White faces is that Black wants to give back the exchange and simplify into an opposite colored bishop endgame. } 3. f4 f6 $1 4. e6 Kf8 $11 { The blockade of the pawns leaves Black with equal chances. } ) 
 ( 1. R4e2 $2 { The retreat would also not have been good, since here Black can play } e4 $11 { when White will never be able to connect his pawns. } ) 
Bxe5 $6 ( 1... Rf8 { may have been the lesser evil, but here White can continue to offer up the exchange and play } 2. Rd1 $16 { with good winning chances due to the extra pawn. } ) 
2. fxe5 $18 { Correct! White is in fact winning here with the bishop pair and pawn structure far outweighing the small material deficit. White's plan will be to move their king up and push the f-pawn to steamroll Black. Here's how that might play out: } Nd3 3. Re3 Nf4 4. d6 Kf8 5. Kh2 Rd7 6. Kg3 Ne6 7. f4 g6 { Black is stuck for options, so he tries to control the f-pawn from pushing further up the board. } 8. h5 Ng7 { Maneuvering the knight to add control over the f5 square. } 9. hxg6 $1 fxg6 ( { White is not worried about } 9... Nf5 { since after } 10. Kf3 Nxe3 11. gxf7 Rxf7 12. Kxe3 $18 { Black's rooks are no match for the bishops + connected pawns. } ) 
10. Kf3 Nf5 11. Rd3 { Black is out of options here, with both rooks passive behind White's pawn mass. } h5 12. Bc4 { Threatening Bb5. } Red8 13. Be6 { There are many ways to win, but this one seems cleanest as the pawns will be quickly forced through. } Rh7 14. Bxf5 gxf5 15. e6 $18 { The pawns will cost Black at least a full rook. Let's take a look at one more example where you can sacrifice the exchange to gain a strong pawn. } 
