 { This game haunted me for a while! I was facing a Grandmaster in the game Astaneh Lopez,A -Jakubowski,K (2017). I sensed that I had an advantage but failed to find the right idea and eventually lost. Can you find the near-winning sequence that I missed? } 18. { -KEY- } Rd7 $1 ( 18. Qc2 $2 { In the game I played too timidly, and after } Qe7 $11 { Black had already escaped danger. } ) 
Bxd7 { Black has to accept the sacrifice, otherwise the c7 pawn would fall. } 19. cxd7 Rd8 20. Nxc7 Rab8 21. Bb5 { Correct! The forcing line has ended. In return for an exchange, White has a typical mix of advantages: the bishop pair and a very strong passed pawn. } Rb7 22. Qc4 $1 Rxd7 { As is usually the case, if the pawn is stable then sooner or later the opponent must give back the exchange for it. If they don't do so, the pawn will likely cost them even more material down the line. } 23. Bxd7 Qd6 24. Ne6 Qxd7 25. Rc1 $16 { White is close to winning, due to the greater activity of all the pieces and the threat of Qc8+. In your own games, if the opportunity to gain some type of strong pawn presents itself it will often be worth taking. In this game White could have forced an advanced passer, while in the previous example Magnus was able to do even better and establish connected pawns in the centre. Let's take a look at one final recurring theme where exchange sacrifices are often best. } 
