 { The last situation that we'll explore where an exchange sacrifice may be called for is when a piece is anchored on a strong outpost. In the game Carlsen,M - Le Tuan,M (2023) we see that both sides have a knight outposted in the centre. Such knights are often just as valuable as a rook, and so the exchange sacrifice should be considered. } 1. { -KEY- } Rxd3 $1 { White correctly eliminates the knight rather than attempting to chase it away. The nice thing about making such a sacrifice is that White's own knight on d6 provides insurance for the missing rook due to its strength. } ( 1. Be2 $2 { would land White in trouble, since Black has a second outpost to maneuver to. } Nb4 $1 2. a3 Nd5 $17 { This outpost is at least as powerful as the d3 outpost. It may be a little less advanced, but it is perfectly central. What's more, it's a 'power outpost' as it is protected by two Black pawns. } ) 
cxd3 2. Qxd3 $13 { The position is unclear, with chances for both sides. } c5 3. Qe4 c4 4. Bd1 $1 { This is the other motivation for the sacrifice. By eliminating the knight, White cleared the b1-h7 diagonal. Now he can look to set up a queen-bishop battery. Already Black is put on the back foot, and it would be easy to make a mistake. } c3 $2 { After this move, White's attack on the kingside builds up quickly. } ( { A sequence such as } 4... Be8 5. Bc2 Bh8 6. a4 $13 { would have led to a continued balanced struggle. } ) 
5. Bc2 Bh8 6. h4 { The pawn break will help to soften up the g5 pawn, setting up future tactics. } a5 { Continuing the march of the pawn majority. Note how White's central pawn and outposted knight make it very difficult for the Black rooks to play an active part in the battle. } 7. Qg4 b4 8. hxg5 hxg5 9. Re1 ( { Defending the e5 pawn, and setting up the threat of } 9. Bh4 { . } ) 
Bg7 10. Qh5 Bh8 ( { Forced. From here White can continue to play ambitiously, with a move like } 10... Bh8 10. f4 { for example. At a minimum, White can play } 11. Qg4 { when Black will have nothing better than to repeat the position. We can see that through the exchange sacrifice, White was able to eliminate the strongest enemy piece. At the same time, the material loss was not a cause for concern since they too have a valuable outposted knight that may - down the line - cost Black one of their own rooks in order to eliminate it. This brings us to a close for this section, and indeed for the chapter. As a quick recap, here are the main themes that we discussed when sacrificing the exchange: 1. The effect on color complexes, usually noticeable when you capture a bishop. 2. The effect on the opponent's structure, such as doubled/ isolated pawns or a weakened kingside. 3. Simplifications & break-throughs. When in a better position, a sacrifice might be needed to clarify the position or to make a decisive break-through. 4. The effect on your structure. Sometimes you will be able to obtain strong pawns such as connected or passed pawns. 5. Outposted pieces. In particular knights on the 6th rank are often worth at least as much as a rook, making them attractive targets to remove. } ) 

